# status/MULTIWORLD-PATCH.diff - the multi-World changes to the four human-owned files.
#
# REGENERATED T138a against REAL MASTER:
#   D:\v100\TERA_SERVER.100\TeraSharp\src\TeraSharp.Arbiter\...   (not the cowork worktree)
#
# APPLY IT WITH:
#
#   cd /d D:\v100\TERA_SERVER.100\TeraSharp
#   git apply --check status\MULTIWORLD-PATCH.diff && git apply status\MULTIWORLD-PATCH.diff
#
# --ignore-whitespace is NOT needed any more. It is harmless if you add it.
#
# WHY THE PREVIOUS REVISIONS DID NOT APPLY - it was never context drift
#
# T138 checked the patch against the COWORK WORKTREE's copies. Those are byte-identical to
# master in content, but their LINE ENDINGS differ:
#
#     file                     master            cowork
#     WorldBridge.cs           LF, 635 lines     CRLF, 635 lines
#     GameSession.cs           LF, 355           CRLF, 355
#     WorldEntry.cs            LF, 316           CRLF, 316
#     HandlerRegistry.cs       LF, 427           CRLF, 420   (+7 lines: the T131/T134 Reg lines)
#
# The whole master tree is LF; the whole worktree is CRLF. A patch generated from CRLF context
# cannot strictly apply to LF files, which is exactly why --ignore-whitespace made three of them
# pass and why the failure looked like drift. T106's LinkCount, T121's DeliverTunnelled lambda and
# T106's LogsAtDebug branch are all present and IDENTICAL in both copies - diffing the two files
# with the endings normalised produces no output at all.
#
# This revision is LF throughout, generated by `git diff` against master, and verified by applying
# it to a pristine copy of the four master files: strict `git apply --check` is clean, and all four
# files stay LF-only with their BOMs intact (WorldBridge, WorldEntry and HandlerRegistry have one;
# GameSession does not).
#
# ONE CORRECTNESS FIX CARRIED OVER FROM T138
#
# The pre-T138 revision's RegisterPlayer hunk still had the pre-T121 context/removal line
#     _tunnels[s.TunnelKey] = new TunnelReorderBuffer { Deliver = s.Send };
# Applying it would have dropped T121's
#     ... { Deliver = p => Handlers.ArbiterClientHandlers.DeliverTunnelled(s, p) };
# which is what injects S_ADMIN_GM_SKILL ahead of the tunnelled S_LOAD_TOPO - the Alt+A fix. Both
# sides of that hunk now carry the T121 form, so applying keeps the injection AND adds the
# (World, Ticket) key.
#
# WHAT IT CONTAINS
#
#   WorldBridge     PerWorld<TicketAllocator> / PerWorld<WorldRuntime>; _tunnels keyed
#                   (World, Ticket); DeliverTunnelPacket / RegisterTunnelRoute /
#                   ResetTunnelSequence take a worldId, each with a DefaultWorldId overload so
#                   every existing caller compiles unchanged.
#   WorldEntry      reads the destination World out of AS_ENTER_WORLD, allocates the Ticket in
#                   THAT World's space and stamps it.
#   GameSession     CurrentWorldId, defaulting to WorldRegistration.DefaultWorldId.
#   HandlerRegistry the call-site updates that follow.
#
# SINGLE-WORLD IS UNCHANGED: every new overload defaults to DefaultWorldId, so with one link
# registered the key is always (0, ticket).
#
# DEPENDENCIES ALREADY IN THE TREE: DungeonRouting.Channels / .Transfers / .WorldForEnterWorld /
# .StampTicket and WorldServerList.SeedDefault all live in the Cowork-owned
# World/WorldInstances.cs and World/WorldServerList.cs.
#
# NOT INCLUDED: the hand-off protocol itself (0x13BE / 0x13BF / 0x13C0 / 0x13C1 / 0x13C5 /
# 0x13C6). Those are pinned byte-for-byte in World/ContinentRouting.cs (T138) and still need their
# call sites wired into WorldBridge.HandleFrame - status/MULTIWORLD-DESIGN.md section T137c step 2.
#
